  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Empowerment, Equality and Education in Rwanda</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The post-genocide environment in Rwanda has increased the incidence of disabilities related to post-traumatic stress. There are also increased incidents of loss of speech and trauma related mental illness in children who have been born since. Despite this, there is little provision for disabled children within the education system in Rwanda. This project will work with a local community based organisation to ensure that disabled children are able to go to school. Children with disabilities and special learning needs in the Northern districts of Musanze, Gakenke and Nayabihu are missing out on access to education as a result of social stigma associated with disability, lack of resources and capacity by the local district government and a lack of awareness around the rights and capabilities of young people with disabilities. This intervention will result in 290 children with hearing impairment and communications disorders and special learning needs being integrated into mainstream schools; 253 school teachers trained in delivering inclusive education; 36 community volunteer learning support assistants, and thousands of children realising an enhanced quality of life through social acceptance and a prosperous transition into adulthood. By the end of the project, it will have developed an example for Rwanda that will be promoted to the government and to other development organisations of how disabled children can be included in education and community life despite the many challenges and lack of funding. Examples of best practice, advice and training will support the broader adoption of this approach, and advocacy, outreach and community events will help to create an environment in which young people with disabilities can claim an equal place in society and build a positive future.</narrative>
  </description>
